All the seven units (appellants herein) are located within the same compound and are managed by a family consisting of Shri R.V. Velankar and his wife, their six daughters and a couple of other relations. The units are staled to be registered under the Shops Act, Companies Act and Factories Act. The Collector after investigating the activities of these units issued a show cause notice to all of them under Rule 9(2) read with Section 11A of the Act, why duty of Rs. 92,53,778.87 should not be demanded from them on cotton fabrics processed by M/s. Sunshine Bleaching House and M/s. Tushar Kanji Kendra during the period 1.11.1982 to 30.3.1987. It was alleged that by not intimating their intention to manufacture excisable goods, manufacturing such goods without obtaining a licence, clearing the same without accounting for in statutory records and without payment of duty they had suppressed the fact deliberately with intent to evade payment of duty. They were also called upon to show cause why penalty should not be imposed under Rules 9(2), 52A and 173Q etc. After considering the reply to show-cause notice and personal hearing (including cross examination of witnesses) the Collector confirmed demand for duty for Rs. 17,62,852.17. He also imposed a penalty of Rs. 1,00,000 on M/s. Gajanan Weaving Mills. He has held the assessee and all the proprietors and partners jointly and severally liable to pay duty and penalty. The appeals are against the Collector's order. The issues involved in the appeals, inter alia, are:

(a)

Holding all the units jointly liable to pay duty and penalty is without jurisdiction;

(b)

cost of processing charges has been determined arbitrarily;

(c)

demand for duty on grey cotton fabrics produced on powerlooms and subjected to plain calendering is erroneous;

(d)

benefit of exemption notifications has not been allowed;

(e)

provisions of Section 11A have been wrongly applied; and,

(f)

limits of show cause notice have been unjustly transgressed.

