article_section.text:
M. GOVINDARAJAN

CA

Payment of excise duty at the time of removal of goods is required by the provisions of the Central Excise Act and rules made thereunder. Clandestine removal is the secret removal of goods without payment of duty. Since this is a revenue loss the Department is keen to identify the clandestine removal. Clandestine removal and evasion of excise duty are serious offences. The assessee is liable to pay the duty for the goods clandestinely removed along with interest. Penal provisions will also be attracted. The obligation is on the part of the Department to prove the clandestine removal with proper evidence and not on assumptions, presumptions and surmises or conjectures. In this article the various issues involved in the clandestine removal have been discussed with reference to decided case laws.

Introduction

1. Rule 4 of the Central Excise Rules, 2002 provides that every person who produces or manufactures any excisable goods or who stores such goods in a warehouse, shall pay the duty leviable on such goods in the manner provided in rule 8 or under any other law, and no excisable goods, on which any duty is payable, shall be removed without payment of duty from any place, where they are produced or manufactured, or from a warehouse, unless otherwise provided. Rule 8 provides the manner of payment of duty. The duty on the goods removed from the factory or the warehouse during a month shall be paid by the 6th day of the following month, if the duty is paid electronically, through internet banking and by the 5th day of the following month, in any other case.

Clandestine removal

2. Clandestine removal literally means secret removal. In the context of the provisions of the Central Excise Act and the Rules it means removal of goods without payment of duty. The same may with the intention to evade payment of duty. The Department may make surprise visits to the units and check the records and registers maintained to ensure that no evasion of duty or clandestine removal of goods has taken place. Where the Department finds it on investigation that the goods have been removed without payment of duty the Department may initiate action against the assessee for such non-payment of duty. In case clandestine removal is proved, the assessee would be liable to pay duty with interest. Penalty is also imposable in such cases. The issues in regard to clandestine removal have been discussed in this article.

Clandestine clearance of goods and evasion of excise duty are serious offences. Such offences cannot be found with adequate evidence. In a case of clandestine removal concrete and cogent evidence is essential. It is essential that the adjudicating authority shall furnish copies of all relied on and unrelied documents to the assessee before adjudicating the allegations. It is also imperative that the witnesses are allowed to be examined by the assessee.

Principle of Natural Justice is to be followed

3. In any judicial or quasi-judicial proceeding reasonable opportunity is to be given to the parties to the case. The same concept is also applicable to clandestine removal.

In Jindal Nickel & Alloys Ltd. v. CCE 2012 (279) ELT 134 (Tri.-Delhi) SS ingots were manufactured by the assessee and sent to job workers for conversion into SS flats. The allegation was excess production and removal of ingots. The Commissioner ordered confiscation of SS ingots but allowed the same to be redeemed on payment of fine. He imposed equal penalty. The assessee filed appeal before the Tribunal. The Tribunal held that prima facie, excess production had not been proved. The Tribunal further held as under—

The Commissioner did not observe principles of natural justice and gave certain findings especially on the capacity of production without giving fair opportunity to the parties;

The Commissioner's refusal to grant cross examination of co-noticee was not legal or proper. Cross examination was to be allowed;

Facts regards capacity of production of assessee were not properly appreciated by Commissioner. For example, Chartered Engineer's certificate was rejected, no. of transformers installed was excessively fixed. The Commissioner was to take a holistic view on capacity of manufacture of assessed;

Evidence of co-noticee against another co-noticee was wrongly relied upon without independent corroboration. Oral evidence was not sufficient to establish a serious charge of clandestine production and removal;

Genuineness of loose slips was to be established by corroborative evidence;

Assessee's plea that he was not manufacturer of the SS flats was to be considered by the Commissioner;

The Tribunal remanded the matter to Commissioner to consider the case afresh.

Obligation of Department to prove clandestine removal

 co-noticee against another co-noticee was wrongly relied upon without independent corroboration. Oral evidence was not sufficient to establish a serious charge of clandestine production and removal;

Genuineness of loose slips was to be established by corroborative evidence;

Assessee's plea that he was not manufacturer of the SS flats was to be considered by the Commissioner;

The Tribunal remanded the matter to Commissioner to consider the case afresh.

Obligation of Department to prove clandestine removal 4. In case of clandestine removal it is the obligation on the part of the Department to prove the clandestine removal with proper and positive evidence. There should be no presumptions and assumptions. The evidence brought forth by the Revenue is to prove the case beyond doubt.

4.1 Relevant case laws

4.1-1 In CCE v. Decent Dyeing Co. 1990 (45) ELT 201 (SC): It was held by the Supreme Court that it is for the department to prove the removal of the goods without payment of duty.

The charge of clandestine removal must be corroborated by independent and un-impeachable evidences, such as purchase of excess new material, excess consumption of electricity, transport and delivery of goods to consignees mentioned in invoices/payment to the manufacturers. The charge of clandestine removal is a serious charge which must be proved by the department by adducing sufficient and tangible evidences. Demand of duty cannot be confirmed on the basis of assumptions and presumptions and surmises and conjectures as was held in CCE v. Lakshmi Engg. Works 2010 (254) ELT 205 (Punj. & Har.).

4.1-2 In Ruby Chlorates (P.) Ltd. v. CCE' 2006 (204) ELT 607 (Tri.-Chennai) : It was held that it is settled legal position that when several raw materials are involved, when a case of clandestine production and clearance is built on clandestine use of raw materials, the same should be proven with reference to unaccounted use of such major raw materials. In a case of clandestine removal the Department should produce positive evidence to establish the same. In the absence of corroborative evidence, a finding cannot be based on the contents of loose sheets of uncertain authority. Failure on the part of the Department to produce evidence of use of inputs to prove that there was manufacture of unaccounted finished product, absence of statements disclosing the source of procurement of the raw materials absence of evidence regarding the use of electricity or receipt of sale consideration by the assessee would establish failure on the part of the Department to bring home the charge of clandestine removal by the manufacturer.

4.1-3 In CCE v. Sangamitra Cotton Mills (P.) Ltd. 2004 (163) ELT 472 (Tri.- Chennai) : The Tribunal noted that the case had been built on the basis of the seized notebook and the file. There was no evidence of—

The assessee committing the offence of removal of goods clandestinely;

Use of inputs to prove that there was manufacture of product;

Clearance of final goods without payment of duty;

Statement as to from whom the appellant had purchased the raw materials;

Use of electricity, receipt of sale consideration to prove the event of manufacture and clearance of goods clandestinely.

The Commissioner (Appeals) had clearly laid down that there had to be concrete evidence to prove the charge of manufacture and sale of goods without payment of duty. In the absence of these evidences, the Commissioner was justified in setting aside the order-in-original. There was no merit in the appeal filed by the Revenue and the same was dismissed.

5. Hypothetical calculation

5.1 Relevant case laws

5.1-1 In Shree Nathajee Industries v. CCE 2011 (267) ELT 241 (Tri. - Ahd.) there was no proof or evidence to show the—

movement of raw material or final product to and from assessee's factory;

use of more electricity, water, labour; and

flow back of money from entities to which raw material was allegedly sent;

There was no inculpatory statement on record of—

proprietors of units from which goods were allegedly removed;

partners of assessee; and

workers of assessee indicating excess manufacture of final products to the extent of more than 100%.

The appellants showed that—

production for years before installation of machine was taken into account;

capacity of machines was taken to be higher;

every week there were two holidays, one being Sunday and other for power, thereby reducing working days to 260 days;

two shifts per day were considered by the department, whereas only one batch could be taken in one day as vessel had to be cooled/cleaned that day; therefore, actual days of production were 130 days;

The Tribunal held that the hypothetical calculation by the Commissioner (Appeals) was incorrect. Evidence should not merely raise doubt against assessee but Revenue's case should be beyond doubt. The Tribunal further held that the finding of clandestine removal was unjustified.

